 ### [Direct, triangle-parallel solid voxelization](9073-direct-triangle-parallel-solid-voxelization.md)
-A data-parallel algorithm for solid voxelization that parallelizes over all triangles, dedicating one thread per triangle to determine which voxels inside a closed, watertight object are set. Choose when maximum CPU/GPU parallelism is required and memory bandwidth is the bottleneck.
+Direct, triangle-parallel solid voxelization is a data-parallel algorithm that determines all voxels inside a closed, watertight mesh by processing every triangle in parallel, dedicating one thread (or thread group) per triangle. It solves the problem of generating a dense voxel grid representation of a mesh on a GPU by projecting triangles onto the YZ plane, testing voxel columns for overlap, and using atomic operations to set the interior bits along the X axis. It differs from its sibling [Tile-Based Solid Voxelization](9087-tile-based-solid-voxelization.md) by grouping work by input primitive (triangles) rather than by spatial location (tiles), which avoids memory bandwidth bottlenecks and atomic serialization costs at the expense of warp underutilization.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot A)$, where $N$ is the number of triangles and $A$ is the average area of the projected triangle in grid cells (number of Y-Z voxels tested per triangle). The inner loop fills X voxels, adding a factor of $G_x$ in the worst case, but typically bounded by the triangle's X-extent [G].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(G_x \cdot G_y \cdot G_z)$ for the voxel table. The algorithm itself uses $O(1)$ auxiliary space per triangle [G].
-- **Measured Performance**: In the reference implementation (CUDA Voxelizer CPU fallback), performance is measured in milliseconds for typical mesh sizes (e.g., thousands of triangles) on a CPU [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Deterministic results; no stochastic noise; efficient for convex or simple concave meshes; leverages 2D projection to reduce 3D complexity [G].
-- **Limitations**: Fails or produces artifacts for triangles parallel to the YZ plane (handled by skipping, potentially causing holes); assumes a consistent winding order; may produce "staircase" artifacts on diagonal surfaces; not suitable for non-manifold meshes without preprocessing [G].
-- **When to use**: When a fast, deterministic CPU-based solid voxelization is needed for meshes with well-defined normals and no extreme degeneracies [G].
-- **When not to use**: For meshes with triangles parallel to the projection plane, non-manifold geometry, or when high-frequency surface detail requires ray-casting accuracy [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(T \cdot B_{yz})$, where $T$ is the number of triangles and $B_{yz}$ is the average number of pixels (voxel columns) in the Y-Z projection bounding box.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$, where $V$ is the total number of voxels in the grid, stored as bits in `voxel_table`.
+*   **Performance:** The method is efficient for CPU execution due to its simple, cache-friendly memory access patterns and lack of complex data structures. However, it is inherently sequential per triangle and does not parallelize well on CPUs without explicit threading (e.g., OpenMP) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Deterministic results, no floating-point precision issues related to ray marching, simple implementation.
+*   **Limitations:** Performance degrades with high triangle counts or large projected bounding boxes. The $O(N^3)$ bit-flipping for solid filling (iterating X for every YZ pixel) can be costly for large volumes.
+*   **When to use:** Small to medium meshes, CPU-only environments, or as a fallback for GPU voxelization [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Large-scale scenes with millions of triangles, or when real-time performance is required on CPU.
 
 ## Background
-The method is based on the Schwarz-Seidel solid voxelization algorithm, which uses 2D projection and edge traversal to fill voxels [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented as a CPU fallback in the CUDA Voxelizer project by Jeroen Baert, providing a deterministic alternative to GPU-based stochastic or ray-casting methods [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm handles edge cases by checking winding order and using specific edge flags to determine voxel inclusion [S1, unofficial].
